+++
title: Hanging Gardens of Babylon
description: One of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World
hook: The Hanging Gardens are the only one of the Seven Wonders whose location
  has never been definitively established, and no Babylonian text mentions
  them at all. One scholar's answer is that they were real, and in the wrong
  city.
standfirst: One of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World, said to have stood
  at Babylon in what is now Iraq, and the only one that may never have been
  built. What survives is five Greek and Roman descriptions, and an argument
  about which city they describe.
key-facts:
  - name: Where it was said to be
    value: Babylon, near present-day Hillah, Babil province, in Iraq
  - name: When it was built
    value: 6th century BC on the Wikidata record; the legend credits
      Nebuchadnezzar II, who ruled between 605 and 562 BC
  - name: Who built it, depending on who you read
    value: Nebuchadnezzar II according to Berossus; a "Syrian king" according to
      Diodorus Siculus and Quintus Curtius Rufus; the legendary queen
      Semiramis in another tradition
  - name: What survives of it
    value: Nothing. No extant Babylonian texts mention the gardens and no
      definitive archaeological evidence has been found in Babylon
  - name: Also called
    value: The Hanging Gardens of Semiramis, after the legendary queen also
      credited with building them
  - name: What it counts as
    value: One of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World; culture, Babylonia
pull-quotes:
  - text: In this palace he erected very high walls, supported by stone pillars;
      and by planting what was called a pensile paradise, and replenishing it
      with all sorts of trees, he rendered the prospect an exact resemblance
      of a mountainous country. This he did to gratify his queen, because she
      had been brought up in Media, and was fond of a mountainous situation.
    attribution: Berossus, writing c. 290 BC, quoted by Josephus
    section: descriptions-in-classical-literature
  - text: Sennacherib king of the world king of Assyria. Over a great distance I
      had a watercourse directed to the environs of Nineveh, joining together
      the waters.... Over steep-sided valleys I spanned an aqueduct of white
      limestone blocks, I made those waters flow over it.
    attribution: Inscription on the aqueduct at Jerwan
    section: identification-with-sennacheribs-gardens-at-nineveh
  - text: a wonder for all peoples
    attribution: Sennacherib, on his new palace and garden
    section: identification-with-sennacheribs-gardens-at-nineveh
timeline:
  - when: 883–859 BC
    what: "King Ashurnasirpal II creates a canal that cuts through the mountains,
      and fruit tree orchards are planted: the Assyrian tradition of royal
      garden building that Sennacherib inherits."
  - when: c. 705/704 – c. 681 BC
    what: Sennacherib reigns as king of Assyria and builds the gardens at his
      palace at Nineveh that Stephanie Dalley identifies with the Hanging
      Gardens.
  - when: 669–631 BC
    what: Ashurbanipal, Sennacherib's grandson, in whose North Palace at Nineveh a
      bas relief shows a luxurious garden watered by an aqueduct.
  - when: c. 645 BC
    what: The "Garden Party" relief is carved in the North Palace at Nineveh,
      showing Ashurbanipal and his wife under a pergola of climbing grapevines
      among date palms and pine trees.
  - when: between 605 and 562 BC
    what: Nebuchadnezzar II rules Babylon; one legend has him build the gardens
      for Queen Amytis.
  - when: 6th century BC
    what: Inception of the Hanging Gardens, on the Wikidata record.
  - when: 331 BC
    what: Before the Battle of Gaugamela, Alexander the Great camps for four days
      near the aqueduct at Jerwan; the historians travelling with him record
      the works in Greek.
  - when: c. 290 BC
    what: Berossus, a Babylonian priest of Marduk, writes the earliest known
      mention of the gardens, and the only one to credit Nebuchadnezzar II
      with them.
  - when: c. 64 BC – 21 AD
    what: Strabo, possibly following the lost account of Onesicritus, says the
      gardens were watered by an Archimedes' screw from the Euphrates.
  - when: c. 60–30 BC
    what: Diodorus Siculus, drawing on Cleitarchus and Ctesias of Cnidus, gives
      the measurements of a square garden built by a "Syrian king".
  - when: c. 37–100 AD
    what: Josephus quotes Berossus's description of the gardens.
  - when: 1st century AD
    what: Quintus Curtius Rufus puts the gardens on a citadel 20 stadia in
      circumference; on the second theory, this is also about when they were
      destroyed.
  - when: 4th to 5th century AD
    what: Philo of Byzantium, the paradoxographer, praises the engineering in A
      Handbook to the Seven Wonders of the World.
  - when: mid-19th century
    what: Austin Henry Layard excavates parts of Sennacherib's palace at Nineveh;
      his citadel plan shows contours consistent with the garden, though its
      position has not been confirmed.
image-plan:
  - file: Hanging_Gardens_of_Babylon.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: "A hand-coloured engraving, probably made in the 19th century after
      the first excavations in the Assyrian capitals: the fabled gardens in
      tiers, with the Tower of Babel behind. An imagined view, not a recorded
      one."
  - file: Ancient_seven_wonders_timeline.svg
    role: inline
    section: lead
    caption: Timeline and map of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World. The
      Hanging Gardens are the one entry whose location has not been
      definitively established.
  - file: Waldeck_Hängende_Gärten_der_Semiramis.jpg
    role: inline
    section: descriptions-in-classical-literature
    caption: Hanging gardens of Semiramis, by H. Waldeck. Construction has also
      been attributed to the legendary queen, and the gardens have been called
      the Hanging Gardens of Semiramis.
  - file: Hanging_Gardens_of_Babylon.gif
    role: full
    section: identification-with-sennacheribs-gardens-at-nineveh
    caption: "A copy of a bas relief from the North Palace of Ashurbanipal
      (669–631 BC) at Nineveh, showing a luxurious garden watered by an
      aqueduct: the contemporary Assyrian picture of a garden that Babylon has
      never produced."
  - file: Gardens_of_Ninevah.png
    role: inline
    section: identification-with-sennacheribs-gardens-at-nineveh
    caption: An Assyrian wall relief of gardens at Nineveh. Several features
      mentioned by the classical authors are discernible on these contemporary
      images.
  - file: Babylon_Babil_Iraq.jpg
    role: inline
    section: plants
    caption: Date palms (Phoenix dactylifera) are a common tree species in
      Babylon, and one of the plants that may have grown in the gardens.
  - file: Assyrian_Relief_of_the_Banquet_of_Ashurbanipal_From_Nineveh_Gypsum_N_Palace_British_Museum_01.jpg
    role: full
    section: plants
    caption: 'The "Garden Party" relief: Ashurbanipal and his wife seated under a
      pergola of climbing grapevines with hanging grapes, small birds among
      them, surrounded with fruiting date palms and pine trees. The head of a
      defeated king hangs between the 1st and 2nd figures at left. North
      Palace, Nineveh, c. 645 BC.'
glossary:
  - term: κρεμαστός (kremastós)
    gloss: The Greek word the gardens are named from, literally 'overhanging';
      broader than the modern English "hanging", it refers to trees planted on
      a raised structure such as a terrace.
  - term: Berossus
    gloss: A Babylonian priest of Marduk whose writing of c. 290 BC is the
      earliest known mention of the gardens and the only source to credit
      Nebuchadnezzar II with them; it survives because Josephus quoted it.
  - term: Archimedes' screw
    gloss: The water-raising device Strabo says carried water from the Euphrates
      up to the gardens; Sennacherib describes the making and operation of
      screws to raise water in his own garden.
  - term: plethron
    gloss: The unit in which Diodorus gives the sides of the garden, approximately
      four to a side. The article gives no modern equivalent.
  - term: cubit
    gloss: "The unit in which Diodorus gives the height of the uppermost gallery:
      50 of them."
  - term: stadion
    gloss: "The unit in which Quintus Curtius Rufus gives the circumference of the
      citadel the gardens stood on: 20 of them."
  - term: bas relief
    gloss: A carved wall panel; the ones from the North Palace of Ashurbanipal at
      Nineveh show a luxurious garden watered by an aqueduct.
  - term: '"lost-wax" process'
    gloss: The casting technique Sennacherib said he was the first to replace,
      with a new method used for his monumental (30 tonne) bronze castings.
further:
  - slug: babylon
    why: The city the gardens were said to have been built in, where no extant
      text mentions them and no definitive archaeological evidence has been
      found.
  - slug: assyria
    why: Sennacherib's kingdom, with its tradition of royal garden building going
      back to Ashurnasirpal II, and Stephanie Dalley's candidate for where the
      gardens really were.
  - slug: euphrates
    why: The river Diodorus says the gardens were irrigated from, and which flowed
      east of its current position in Nebuchadnezzar II's time, possibly over
      whatever evidence survives.
